 "productionNotes": "No source fetched gives a confirmed total, and the per-variant counts only partly agree. Wikipedia states 65 Agera-family cars for 2010 to 2018 without a breakdown. Supercars.net lists seven Agera, 18 Agera R (2011 to 2014), the One:1 as six cars plus one prototype, 25 Agera RS (2015 to 2018) and three Agera Final, plus an Agera S count that no second source confirms; its lines add up to 65 only when that Agera S count and the prototype are included and the RS is 25. RM Sotheby's independently supports two of the lines: one of just seven examples of the original Agera, and only 18 Agera R built from 2011 to 2014. The One:1 count of six customer cars is supported by Wikipedia and both Supercars.net pages, and the three-car Agera Final by GTspirit's report of the factory announcement. The conflict: Motor Authority and Auto123 (reports from 2015 and 2016, the earlier one describing a plan) and Supercars.net give 25 Agera RS, while Wikipedia states 27 were built. The Agera S has a single listed count and no second source, so no Agera S number is printed here. For that reason productionTotal is null. No source fetched gives a count of cars built to U.S. specification or federalized for the American market, by variant or in total: the registry says only that one 2013 car was the first of two U.S.-spec Ageras and was never delivered here, RM Sotheby's says its 2014 Agera R was not built to U.S. specifications, and Wikipedia says one bespoke car is the only Agera S in the United States. The factory's own heritage records were not reached. A U.S. new price is documented only by press reports of differing dates and cars (see the specs and market notes); no dealer price sheet was found.",
